Date: Wed, 01 Dec 1999 08:55:28 +0000
Subject: Re: [S] Variance components in GLMs with random factors
In-Reply-To: <C1B1A78EB953D211902D0040056AC48B045309@VRVCOM>
References: <C1B1A78EB953D211902D0040056AC48B045309@VRVCOM>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.05.9912010846580.3268-100000@auk.stats>

On Tue, 30 Nov 1999, Pierre Delfosse wrote:

> In GLM, if Yi are the observations and L the link function, L(E(Yi)) is
> supposed to be a linear combination
>  of the covariates.  The unknown coefficients of this combination can be
> estimated using the 'glm' function.  If some or all 
> of the factors are random, one could be interested in the variances
> associated to them in place of the individual coefficients
>  values.  The function 'varcomp' do that for linear models.  What can we
> do in the GLM case?

If some of the factors are random, this is no longer a GLM but a GLMM,
and the GLM estimates are no longer applicable.  There is not much
software around to fit GLMMs in S, although for some specific GLMMs
some does exist.

(The same thing happens with linear models: lme is much more complicated
than lm. varcomp only applies to a particular and restrictive form of
random effects.)

