From ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk  Wed Dec  1 09:07:26 1999
From: ripley at stats.ox.ac.uk (Prof Brian D Ripley)
Date: Wed, 01 Dec 1999 09:07:26 +0000
Subject: Re: [S] Standard S?
In-Reply-To: <19991201172105W.masao@nf.enveng.titech.ac.jp>
References: <19991201172105W.masao@nf.enveng.titech.ac.jp>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.05.9912010855520.3268-100000@auk.stats>

On Wed, 1 Dec 1999, UEBAYASHI 'UMA' Masao wrote:

> 
> Is S standardized?
> Does the specification exist???

Not standardized in the sense that C or Fortran is, by an International
Standards body (so it is ISO C, strictly). However, as there are only a
handful of implementations available, that is not particularly important.
One could argue that current S is defined by Chambers (1998) book
`Programnming with Data', but that certainly does not define current S-PLUS
accurately.

There are many points at which what the S books say happens and what S-PLUS
actually does differs, which makes writing about this rather tedious if one
wants to be accurate.  (I suspect that rather little of this is
attributable to MathSoft changes to S.)  Try to understand method dispatch
in S version 3 from the books and then try some examples if you want an
(extremely frustrating) example.  Or look at Chambers (1998), see
complex objects in S map to complex object in Fortran, and then try it
(they map to double complex, in fact).  These are precisely the sort of
things that standard committees have to spend a lot of time (and money)
resolving.

