Bert Gunter wrote, on 28 Nov 1999:

> I recently spent longer than I wish to publicly acknowledge tracking down
> the following S-Plus "bad" behavior. Well, not really -- more my ignorance
> of normal S-Plus behavior. Now while Brian (Ripley) may point out that I
> should have known -- and I agree, I should have -- the fact is that I
> didn't, and it certainly wasn't something that sticks in my mind as widely
> publicized. [...]
>
> It's those darn row.names [...that increase execution time by 10x].

Thanks to Bert for that posting.  May I disagree about "should have 
known"?  The idea that good software is filled with traps that the user 
"should" avoid is a relatively recent and (I would say) ill-considered 
one.  The user should write code that is logically correct.  Good software 
should handle mundane tasks, freeing the user for work of substance.  The 
behavior mentioned is a *flaw in*, not a useful feature of, S-Plus.

(Those who agree with my argument may be interested in the latest issue of 
the US publication _Business_ _Week_.  The cover story, about buggy 
software, is entitled "Software Hell.")

