Date: Wed, 01 Dec 1999 17:35:16 -0800
Subject: Re: [S] sample selection with PPS
In-Reply-To: <383AD216.C1FF6196@istat.it>
References: <383AD216.C1FF6196@istat.it>
Message-ID: <199912020135.RAA18942@quake.statsci.com>

Marcello D'Orazio wrote:
>is there someone out there that knows how function 
>"sample" works when you want to extract a sample with 
>Probability Proportional to Size (PPS) without replacement?
>When I say how works..., I mean which kind of method for the 
>selection of sample units, i.e.
>
>> sample(N=6, n=3, replace=F, prob=c(10, 20, 15, 30, 35, 50) )

Correction, that should be one of:
sample(x=  6, size=3, replace=F, prob=c(10, 20, 15, 30, 35, 50) )
sample(x=1:6, size=3, replace=F, prob=c(10, 20, 15, 30, 35, 50) )

The algorithm is essentially
for(i in 1:size)
  sample an observation from those not yet sampled, with probabilities
  proportional to the original probabilities (excluding those probabilities
  for observations already chosen)

Note that the overall selection probabilities are not proportional to
the specified probabilities -- the actual probabilities are more
nearly uniform.  Similarly, the selection probabilities vary at
different steps.  The following demonstrates this:

results <- matrix(NA, 2, 1000)
set.seed(0)
for(i in 1:1000)
  results[,i] <- sample(1:3, 2, replace=F, prob=c(.5, .3, .2))
dimnames(results) <- list( c("first","second"), NULL)
apply(results, 1, tabulate, nbins=3)

     first second 
[1,]   508    342
[2,]   304    378
[3,]   188    280

The selections at the first step are proportional to the specified
probs, but the selection probabilities at the second step are different;
item 1 has a much smaller chance of being selected because it
has a high probability of already having been selected.

If anyone knows of an algorithm for efficiently sampling
with replacement with specified unconditional probabilities
please send me the reference or details on the algorithm.
(I have derived an algorithm but it is slow.)

