From B.Rowlingson at lancaster.ac.uk  Thu Dec  2 10:45:54 1999
From: B.Rowlingson at lancaster.ac.uk (Baz)
Date: Thu, 02 Dec 1999 10:45:54 +0000
Subject: [S] PS files from irregular grid image() plots
Message-ID: <38464DE2.24814D4E@lancs.ac.uk>

 I've still not heard from MathSoft about the problem with PostScript
output
of images from irregular grids. Seems I wasn't the first to complain
about it
either - I had an email from someone telling me they told MathSoft in
1996.

 So I thought I'd sort it out myself. R does it by drawing a rectangle
for
each matrix element (which does make the resulting output PS file rather
large), but I thought for Splus that approach would require mapping
polygon
colours to image colours. Instead I wrote a function that calls image()
for each matrix element. Here's the code - feel free to modify as you
wish,
to do axis labels or whatever. This is a quick hack.

"myimage"  <- 
function(x,y,z)
{
  n_length(x)
  m_length(y)
  zdims_dim(z)
  if(n-1 != zdims[1]){
    stop("X vector wrong size - must be dim(z)[1]+1")
  }
  if(m-1 != zdims[2]){
    stop("Y vector wrong size - must be dim(z)[2]+1")
  }
  xl_rep(x[-n],m-1)
  yl_rep(y[-m],rep(n-1,m-1))
  xr_rep(x[-1],m-1)
  yr_rep(y[-1],rep(n-1,m-1))
  plot(range(x),range(y),type='n')
  zv_as.vector(z)
  for(ix in 1:length(xl)){
    if(!is.na(zvm_matrix(zv[ix],1,1))){
      image(x=c(xl[ix],xr[ix]),
            y=c(yl[ix],yr[ix]),
            z=zvm,add=T,zlim=range(z,na.rm=T))
    }
  }
}

 Usage is myimage(x,y,z) where x and y have to be one larger than the
dimensions
of z, and specify the bounds of the image pixels. z can have NAs, in
which case 
nothing is drawn at that point. This is very useful in our case since we
have a
very thin, long island on a diagonal. 80% of the image can be set to NA,
speeding
up drawing and printing.

 Resulting images and PostScript files may be very large and may take
some time to 
print or preview.

 Barry Rowlingson
 Computing Officer
 Maths and Stats, Lancaster University, UK
