From zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu  Thu Dec  2 15:15:07 1999
From: zaslavsk at hcp.med.harvard.edu (Alan Zaslavsky)
Date: Thu, 02 Dec 1999 10:15:07 -0500
Subject: Re: [S] apply function
In-Reply-To: <004c01bf3ccb$2764ac40$66a0dcc3@miss-jenny.mpl.ird.fr>
Message-ID: <199912021515.KAA13873@nightingale.hcp.med.harvard.edu>

> From: "Eric Elguero" <Eric.Elguero at mpl.ird.fr>
> I am using S-plus #3.3 under Windows-95 and having trouble with the apply 
> function.
> 
> sbl is a data frame which contains numeric and character columns and I want to
> extract those columns which are numeric, so I thought of something like
> 
> sbl[,apply(sbl,2,is.numeric)]
> 
> unfortunately this does'nt work, see below::
...

What is actually going on here is a bit complicated.  apply() is designed
for matrices, not data frames.  To work on a data frame, it uses
as.matrix() to make it a matrix, which turns it into character data (the
only fully general way of coercing a data frame into a single mode of
data).  Hence whatever column you look at with apply(), it is character.
However, mean() (unlike other summary functions like sum()) will try to
coerce its data to numeric.  The numeric columns have been turned into
character data that can be coerced back to numeric, but the others don't
look like numbers so you get the warnings and NA values.

However, do not despair -- what you want can be done very easily with lapply()
(which operates on lists, of which data frames are a special case).  E.g.

> X_data.frame(aa=letters,bb=1:26)
> unlist(lapply(X,mode))
        aa        bb 
 "numeric" "numeric"
> unlist(lapply(X,is.factor))
 aa bb 
  T  F

Note that a factor is numeric data, so "mode" or "is.numeric" will not do
quite what you want.
