From pierre.delfosse at centexbel.be  Fri Dec  3 09:22:03 1999
From: pierre.delfosse at centexbel.be (Pierre Delfosse)
Date: Fri, 03 Dec 1999 10:22:03 +0100
Subject: [S] RE: S+ software for GLMM models
Message-ID: <C1B1A78EB953D211902D0040056AC48B04531F@VRVCOM>

Many thanks to the persons who responded to my question:

        > In GLM, if Yi are the observations and L the link function,
L(E(Yi)) is
        > supposed to be a linear combination
        >  of the covariates.  The unknown coefficients of this
combination can be
        > estimated using the 'glm' function.  If some or all 
        > of the factors are random, one could be interested in the
variances
        > associated to them in place of the individual coefficients
        >  values.  The function 'varcomp' do that for linear models.
What can we
        > do in the GLM case?

Their answers are summarised hereafter.
------------------------------------------------------------------------
----------------

Prof. Brian Ripley:

        If some of the factors are random, this is no longer a GLM but a
GLMM,
        and the GLM estimates are no longer applicable.  There is not
much
        software around to fit GLMMs in S, although for some specific
GLMMs
        some does exist.

        (The same thing happens with linear models: lme is much more
complicated
        than lm. varcomp only applies to a particular and restrictive
form of
        random effects.)

------------------------------------------------------------------------
-----------------

Karin Oudshoorn:

        I am working also on a glmm problem and for the moment I will
try to use
        MIXOR (from Hedeker) and Winbugs. IN the forthcoming SAS version
        8.0 there will be a procedure to handle glmm's (PROC NLMIXED).
        In GENSTAT there is also a procedure that handles
        glmm's. (See the artikel of Engel, Keen in Statistica
        Neerlandica 48 nr. 1 "A simple approach for the
        analysis of generalized linear mixed models". Unfortunately
GENSTAT is not a
        very spread program.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
---------------------

David Evans:

        I haven't used it, but the SAS macro 'Glimmix' might be what you
need.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
---------------------

Rolf Turner

        Murray Aitkin has written software in GLIM4 to analyze
generalized
        linear mixed models, with the emphasis being on modelling
        overdispersion through random effects.  The ideas are discussed
in
        his paper in ``A general maximum likelihood analysis of
        overdispersion in generalized linear models'', Statistics and
        Computing, vol. 6, 1996, pp. 251-262.

        The GLIM4 macros to implement the ideas can be found at the URLs

        http://www.mas.ncl.ac.uk/~nma9/allvc.txt   (variance component
macro)
        http://www.mas.ncl.ac.uk/~nma9/alldist.txt  (overdispersion
macro)

        It might well be a useful exercise for someone who speaks both
GLIM
        and Splus (does such a person exist?) to translate Prof.
Aitkin's
        software into Splus.

         

