From Michael.Wing at orst.edu  Fri Dec  3 16:55:02 1999
From: Michael.Wing at orst.edu (Wing, Michael)
Date: Fri, 03 Dec 1999 08:55:02 -0800
Subject: RE: [S] data frame manipulation question
In-Reply-To: <6B677297622ED311A8E100902785F93106AD7F@ribes.for.orst.edu>
Message-ID: <6B677297622ED311A8E100902785F93106AD80@ribes.for.orst.edu>

Hi All,

Samuel Buttrey responded to my question 10 minutes after I posted it to the
S-Plus list.  His answer (and anticipation of my needing to address NAs) was
right on the mark.  I include my question and Samuel's response below-
thank you Samuel.  Michael Wing.

> -----Original Message-----

> Hi all,
> 
> I have a data frame that is organized in the following format:
> 
> Obs   T       C       K
> 1     5       8       2
> 2     4       7       6
> 3     3       na      9
> 
> "Obs" is a the observation number while T, C, and K are numeric variables.
> 
> I would like to output a data frame (or other object that I can convert
> into
> a text file) that captures every unique combination of each of the three
> variables.  The desired output would look like (18 possibilities in this
> example):
> 
> obs   T       C       K
> 1     5       8       2
> 2     5       8       6
> 3     5       8       9
> 4     4       8       2
> 5     4       8       6
> .
> .
> 18    3       7       9       
> 
> 
> Can this be accomplished in S-Plus?  I am using S-Plus 2000 for Windows
> NT.
> 

RESPONSE FROM SAMUEL BUTTREY: 

Subject: RE: [S] data frame manipulation question

Hi.  expand.grid() will do almost exactly what you want. Try
expand.grid(<your data frame>)

The problem is, it will expand the NA's as well. I guess that's not what you
want. So you might create a list with one item per column of your data
frame,  from which you have removed NA's:

expand.grid(lapply (zz, function (x) x[!is.na(x)]))

This will do what you want, I believe.

I hope this helps,

Sam Buttrey
buttrey at nps.navy.mil
