From jjthaden at flash.net  Mon Dec  6 06:18:12 1999
From: jjthaden at flash.net (John Thaden)
Date: Mon, 06 Dec 1999 00:18:12 -0600
Subject: [S] A subset() function that sidesteps the data.frame() bug.
Message-ID: <3.0.3.32.19991206001812.00715488@pop.flash.net>

     As I noted yesterday, the data.frame() function has the unpleasant
side effect of converting logical vectors and list elements to factor
columns, at least in S-Plus 4.5.  Among other things, this affects the
handy function, subset(), authored by  Bradley Biggerstaff and Tony Plate,
and an enhancement of it by Bill Venables called df.subset() [archives, 7
Oct. 1997].
     At the expense of looping, here is a version that doesn't corrupt
logical columns:
>subset <- function(x, ...)
     UseMethod("subset")
> subset.data.frame <- function(x, subset = T)
{
# Written 5 Dec. 1999 by John Thaden.
# Method Function for a generic function subset().
# Picks a subset of a data.frame and adjusts factor
# levels, while avoiding S-Plus functions like
# data.frame() that convert logical columns to factors.
# Acknowledgements:  Bradley Biggerstaff and Tony
# Plate, for the original subset() function, and Bill
# Venables, for the enhanced function df.subset(), both
# of which unfortunately employ data.frame().  Can
# anyone provide a vectorize version?
# Usage example:  
#    lm(y~f1+I(L1*f2), data=subset(df, f1!="red"))
# does regression on a subset of df.  factors f1 and f2
# both will have adjusted levels. L1 will remain a
# logical vector, not be converted to a factor.
#
        subset <- eval(substitute(subset), x)
        x <- x[subset,  ]
        x1 <- names(x)[sapply(x,is.factor)]
        for(i in x1)
        {
                lev <- levels(x[, i])
                x[, i] <- factor(x[, i], lev[is.element(lev, unique(x[, i]))])
        }
        x
}

