From Cougar_711 at msn.com  Wed Dec  8 23:09:58 1999
From: Cougar_711 at msn.com (Frank R. Lawrence)
Date: Wed, 08 Dec 1999 17:09:58 -0600
Subject: [S] re:lme
Message-ID: <NDBBJLJFHLBJLCELPIJEMENMCBAA.Cougar_711@msn.com>

In response to my question regarding standard errors in lme, I received two
responses.  I provide a copy of the responses below.

My Question: I believe I have specified a random intercept
model with subject nested within site. I don't understand why I get such
large SE estimates using the two lme formulas below.  I get two different
estimates of fixed effects with wide variation in the SE estimates.  In
fact, the result that appears closest to reality is the one with largest SE
(the first one), yet the coefficients and SE for time are too large.  I get
the same estimates using REML.  Why are the SEs so large and the intercept
and parameter estimates different? I believe the differences may be caused
by use of the "identity function".

Professor Ripley:

poly uses orthogonal polynomials, so the first two and the last one
are different models.  Although you appear to have lost all the
nice formatting, I don't see that the SE's are `so large': it is the DF and
t values that are large.

Professor Bates:

They are different because the poly function produces orthogonal
polynomials.  For example,
 > poly(1:10, 3)
                 1           2          3
  [1,] -0.49543369  0.52223297 -0.4534252
  [2,] -0.38533732  0.17407766  0.1511417
  [3,] -0.27524094 -0.08703883  0.3778543
  [4,] -0.16514456 -0.26111648  0.3346710
  [5,] -0.05504819 -0.34815531  0.1295501
  [6,]  0.05504819 -0.34815531 -0.1295501
  [7,]  0.16514456 -0.26111648 -0.3346710
  [8,]  0.27524094 -0.08703883 -0.3778543
  [9,]  0.38533732  0.17407766 -0.1511417
 [10,]  0.49543369  0.52223297  0.4534252
 attr(,"degree")
 [1] 1 2 3
 attr(,"coefs")
 attr(,"coefs")$alpha
 [1] 5.5 5.5 5.5

 attr(,"coefs")$norm2
 [1]    1.0   10.0   82.5  528.0 3088.8

The fitted models using wj22 ~ poly(time, 3) and wj22 ~ time +
I(time^2) + I(time^3) are equivalent (assuming 'time' is a numeric
variable but then I don't understand why poly(time, 3) and
poly(as.numeric(time), 3) produce different standard errors for the
intercept) but the coefficients and their standard errors are quite
different, as you have seen.

The advantage of orthogonal polynomials is that you can do the
sequential tests for the order of the polynomial using the t
statistics.  Also they have better numerical properties than the
design generated by wj22 ~ time + I(time^2) + I(time^3).
Interpretation of the coefficients is entirely different, however.

