From kjhealy at princeton.edu  Fri Dec 10 18:51:56 1999
From: kjhealy at princeton.edu (Kieran Healy)
Date: Fri, 10 Dec 1999 13:51:56 -0500
Subject: [S] [summary] S-plus and linux
Message-ID: <38514BCC.220ED573@princeton.edu>

Thanks to Z. Todd Taylor, Prof. Brian Ripley, A.J. Rossini and Alberto
Mura for their help on this question.

My original post had three questions. 

--- 1 ---
>  1. Posts in the S-archive from January of this year suggest that S under
>  linux is a bit "rougher" than under windows or other unix platforms (eg,
>  some found the help system and command line editing, etc, to be
>  unsatisfactory). Is this still the case, or has a newer, wrinkle-free
>  version been released? (It's hard to tell from Mathsoft's site.)

Z. Todd Taylor:
> Splus 5.* is a bit rougher than the 3.* series (on which the
> Win32 Splus 4.* series is based) in the sense that it is a
> thorough redesign of the S language and rewrite of the code.
> Some default behaviors have changed, a few old constructs are not
> forwardly compatible, etc.  But all in all, it is running
> great on my P3-550 Dell box.

Prof Brian Ripley:
> That was January. S-PLUS 5.1 release 1 (ca June) is fine on Linux,
> and has a completely different help system. Command-line editing is still
> not there, but will be in the next version, I am told.

--- 2 ----
> 2. Does anyone have experience installing and running S-plus under
> Caldera OpenLinux 2.3? A Mathsoft post in the archive noted that the
> S-plus for Linux was developed under Red Hat. Are headaches to be
> expected with a different distribution?

No respondents were using this distribution, but some were running other
distributions with no difficulties.

Alberto Mura: 
> I run S-Plus under SUSE Linux 6.0 without any problems at all.

A.J. Rossini:
> I'm running it under Debian.  I think a bit of work may be required,
> but most distn's are straightforward (if you understand the
> differences). 

Prof. Brian Ripley:
> No. Haven't tried Caldera, but apart from a peculiar Fortran install
> (it is complete, won't build R) it should be fine.

--- 3 ---
> 3. Maybe this is obvious, but does S for linux run with a proper
> graphical interface? I use the KDE desktop environment that comes with
> Caldera, and ideally I'd like S-plus to integrate properly with it, and
> look and run like my copy of 4.5 Pro for windows  --- only better, and
> without the occasional crashes :-) 

I should have been clearer in this question. The answer is that S-Plus
for linux doesn't have all the canned menus that 4.5 for windows does,
but of course does implement all the usual graphics capabilities using
X11 windows, as in the unix version. I never use most of the menus
anyway (I wonder if anyone does?) and will probably be using xemacs to
write script files and edit the S-plus command line. So no problems
there.

Prof. Ripley put the question of what constitutes a ``proper graphical
interface'' nicely:
> Yes, a proper graphical interface, that is a command line in a standard
> terminal and a separate X11 graphics windows in a separate process. In
> short, nothing like S-PLUS 4.x, thank goodness.

Thanks again for the quick and informative responses.

Kieran

