In-Reply-To: <385257d5.19384221@julian.uwo.ca>
References: <385257d5.19384221@julian.uwo.ca>
Message-ID: <Pine.GSO.4.05.9912102012440.11415-100000@auk.stats>

On Fri, 10 Dec 1999, Duncan Murdoch wrote:

> 
> Kieran Healy said:
> 
> >Prof. Ripley put the question of what constitutes a ``proper graphical
> >interface'' nicely:
> >> Yes, a proper graphical interface, that is a command line in a standard
> >> terminal and a separate X11 graphics windows in a separate process. In
> >> short, nothing like S-PLUS 4.x, thank goodness.

(I should add that I meant for S-PLUS programming, not for exploration by
beginners.)

> I agree that the S-PLUS for Windows interface is pretty awful, but I
> don't think the command line interface is very good either.  I think
> the following features should be in any modern programming interface.
> (I've been told that an Emacs mode provides some of these; maybe
> StatSci should just copy what Emacs does so everyone gets these
> features):

If you really want all this interference, try ESS under Emacs.

>  - You should be able to edit code in place; the teletype/batch file
> submission model is a real pain to use.  S-PLUS for Windows script
> windows come sort of close to this, but:
> 
>  - Errors should move the cursor to the likely location of the error.

Most Emacs modes do this.
 
>  - The editor should be sensitive to the syntax of the language, doing
> automatic indents and parenthesis and brace matching, syntax
> highlighting, etc.  (The script window does some of this.)

ESS is very much better than the script window at this.  I agree, this is
really helpful, worth learning Emacs for.

>  - The editor should provide syntax help.  If you type a function and
> can't remember the argument list to it, the editor should remind you.
> 
>  - The debugger should be integrated with the editor, so you can
> single step your way through a program, and examine the current values
> of variables without having to execute code to print them.

What debugger? debugger() or inspect()? I think you are describing a
source-code debugger (like inspect) not an integrated system.

> These are all standard features of modern PC programming environments
> for compiled languages, and they really help.  S-PLUS is different in
> that it is an interactive calculator, so the "source code" isn't
> static as it is with a compiled program, but that only means that
> these things need to be done slightly differently, not that they
> shouldn't be done at all, in a "proper graphic interface".

I have yet to see a PC programming environment that is as effective as
those I used on Unix 10 years ago, but that is a matter of taste, of
course. You can't possibly be describing Visual Studio, probably the most
widespread such PC environment, nor the Watcom IDE.

