From fharrell at virginia.edu  Sat Dec 11 12:58:38 1999
From: fharrell at virginia.edu (Frank E Harrell Jr)
Date: Sat, 11 Dec 1999 07:58:38 -0500
Subject: [S] Some thoughts on Emacs, GUI, and LaTeX
Message-ID: <38524A7D.A64F16EA@virginia.edu>

I use ESS with NT GNU Emacs all the time and find it invaluable even though I 
don't
take advantage of it's ability of link "live" with an S-PLUS session.  The more 
I use
Emacs the more in wonder I am about this program and its extensibility.  To me 
the
real reason to learn Emacs is to use it with LaTeX - its AUCTeX and RefTeX modes
for dealing with large complex documents are incredible.  Once you get hooked on
Emacs for this purpose, use it for ESS!  Before long you will also be using 
Emacs
for telnet sessions, ftp, directory navigation, and the command window 
(scrolling,
history, recall, etc.).

Speaking of directory navigation many users will be astounded about the
productivity of Emacs as compared with Windows Explorer.  You can
for example have Emacs mark all files containing "ANOVA" at the
beginning of some line and then easily visit all of those marked files.  You 
can have
Emacs change every occurence of "ANOVA" to "MANOVA" in every
file in the directory (with prompts for confirmation).  This assumes that
files are in plain ASCII (not binary such as Word .doc files), which is
true for me because my files are either .txt, .tex, or .s files.

With RefTeX under Emacs you can easily navigate cross-references and
index entries in large documents.  The latest RefTeX allows you to
provide an "index phrases table" so that you can interactively index
hundreds of phrases of interest in a compound document make up of
a dozen chapter files.  The phrases table provides the default text for
indexing each match.

I only use the Statistics menus under S-PLUS 2000 for sample size and power
calculations (GUIs are wonderful for this).  But I am not as negative on the
S-PLUS GUI as some users as I find it extremely handy for importing and
exporting data files, graphics, and report windows.

For all of our department's Windoze/NT users I have packaged the complete Emacs 
distribution
with the latest ESS, AUCTeX, RefTeX, etc. for use with FPTeX (a terrific free 
version of
LaTeX for Win/NT - thanks to Brian Ripley for informing me about FPTeX) and
Cygnus cygwin unix utilities (especially the bash shell).
Emacs can then be installed and ready to use with a single unzip, if you also
like my .emacs file.

Based on comments I received on s-news from several teachers I am
emboldened to consider requiring my students to learn LaTeX in their first 
semester.

--
