From Peter_Ellis at ausaid.gov.au  Tue Dec 14 00:13:42 1999
From: Peter_Ellis at ausaid.gov.au (Peter Ellis)
Date: Tue, 14 Dec 1999 10:13:42 +1000
Subject: Summary Re:[S] clusplot
Message-ID: <0000A715.C21005@ausaid.gov.au>

Original question:

The documentation for clusplot states that it plots data in 2 dimensions using
"principle components or multidimensional scaling".  I wish to know under what
circumstances it uses which, and whether there is a way to force it to use
multidimensional scaling for data that could be analysed using principle
components but for whatever reason I prefer MDS.

(S-Plus 2000)

* * * * * *

Greg Arnold and Matthew Kurbat both pointed out (thanks) that the first n
dimensions from metric multidimensional scaling working on a euclidean distance
matrix gives effectively the same result as the first n principle components. 
So the answer would seem to be (I am deducing this) that clusplot uses cmdscale
when it is given a distance expression rather than a full matrix of variables
(there is a logical flag to tell it whether it has been given distances or the
full data).  clusplot based on cmdscale will only give a different answer to
that based on principle components if the distance expression is based on
non-euclidean distances.

BTW, in order to do what I wanted, which I now realise is to use non-metric
multidimesnional scaling, I have to use one of the two functions in the V&R MASS
library for this purpose and construct my own plot.

cheers and ta
PE

