From d.gadd at campden.co.uk  Tue Dec 14 11:33:46 1999
From: d.gadd at campden.co.uk (Gadd, Daniel)
Date: Tue, 14 Dec 1999 11:33:46 +0000
Subject: [S] as.integer inside other S-Plus functions
Message-ID: <72039CBD75C9D211AFEF0000F80838DC6F11E8@NTSERV2.campden.co.uk>

Hi S-Pluser's,
  I am hoping that some of you may be able to help. I was asked to help
someone debug a function that they were writing in which the function rep
was used in the following way. (By the way I am using S-Plus 2000 release 1
on a Windows NT4 service Pack 3 machine)

 rep(1, 14.2/0.1) 

the length of this vector (given by length(rep(1,14.2/0.1))) was 141. I
understand that the reason that this is so is due to the floating point
representation of the value 14.2/0.1. If I open up the result of 14.2/0.1 in
the object explorer it is represented as 141.99999999999997000 (double
precision). The function rep must (it is not that obvious) contain a call to
a function similar to as.integer which then converts 14.2/0.1 to an integer,
giving 141. Again, this is documented in the help file for as.integer. The
obvious (to me) solution to this is to use rep in this way:-

rep(1,round(14.2/0.1))  

We also established that the use of as.character inside the rep function
(i.e. rep(1,as.character(14.2/0.1))) also worked. Although I have not
investigated whether this was just in this case or a general rule.

The question that I would like an answer to is:-
        Is there a list of functions which contain an internal call to
as.integer (or an equivalent) so that I can be aware of when I must make
sure that the argument to a function is truly an integer ? 

If not, any other comments on the problem I have encountered would be
welcome.

Thanks in advance,
 Dan

