From etptupaf at bs.ehu.es  Tue Dec 14 14:46:10 1999
From: etptupaf at bs.ehu.es (F.Tusell)
Date: Tue, 14 Dec 1999 15:46:10 +0100
Subject: [S] Mixing S-Plus output and LaTeX
Message-ID: <199912141446.PAA01845@agesi.bs.ehu.es>

I have written a  set of notes for use of my  students a set of notes,
which every  now and then includes  short pieces of S-Plus  code. I am
now  shifting towards  R, which  provides my  students with  a no-cost
alternative. In so doing, I have to check that all examples for S-Plus
remain valid  for R. Since both  are moving targets,  this is becoming
difficult.

Including PostScript  graphics in LaTeX is made  efficient by commands
such as

   \DeclareGraphicsRule{eps.gz}{epe}{eps.bb}{`gzip -d #1}

which (in Linux  at least) enable to keep  PostScript files compressed
and unzip them  on the fly when the document  which references them is
LaTeX'ed.  I have  tried to dissect that command to  see how it works,
with no success.  The goal was to have all code  referenced in text in
external files, run S-Plus on those  files at the time the document is
LaTeX'ed and include the output in the document. Doing so, each time a
new version  of the  document is prepared,  all code  is automatically
re-run and checked. S-Plus and  R variants could be managed by if-then
rules allowing easy creation of different versions of the document for
S-Plus and R.

As I said, I have not been able to make this work. My present approach
--rather clumsy,  I admit-- is to use  "make" to run S-Plus  (or R) on
examples, cut  the top  lines (welcoming flag,  copyright declaration,
version number...) and save the  output for later inclusion into LaTeX
documents. I  wonder if there is a  better way, along the  lines of my
failed attempt to run S-Plus (or R) on the fly.

ft.
